# Installing Herold

Herold is a single static binary. This guide covers the two ways to run
it that are supported today -- a Docker container or a build from source
-- and walks through the first-run bootstrap that takes you from an
installed binary to a working mailbox.

Read [What you need](#what-you-need) and [The system
config](#the-system-config) first, then follow **one** of [Install with
Docker](#install-with-docker) or [Install from source](#install-from-source)
-- each is a complete path on its own. Both finish at [First
run](#first-run).

For day-2 operation see [Operating Herold](operate); for domain and user
administration see [Administering Herold](administer); for the
real-domain DNS walkthrough see _Going Live_.

## What you need

Herold runs on a single Linux host. Recommended sizing:

| Dimension | Minimum | Recommended | Notes                            |
|-----------|---------|-------------|----------------------------------|
| CPU       | 2 vCPU  | 8 vCPU      | LLM spam scoring dominates at scale |
| RAM       | 4 GB    | 32 GB       | The full-text index benefits from RAM |
| Disk      | 50 GB   | 1 TB+ NVMe  | Mail volume, search index, ACME state |
| Network   | 10 Mbps | 100 Mbps    | Outbound bursts plus IMAP IDLE   |

The minimum is comfortable for a personal server with a handful of
mailboxes. Scale RAM with the size of the search index and disk with
mail volume; CPU rarely saturates outside spam classification, which is
a call to a separate plugin rather than herold's own work.

Herold does not encrypt data at rest. If you need disk-level
confidentiality, run on an encrypted filesystem (LUKS, ZFS native
encryption).

## The system config

{%# REQ-OPS-02 #%}

Herold reads everything it needs from one file, **`system.toml`**. It
declares the network listeners, the storage backend, TLS, and any
plugins. Both install paths below need this file in place before the
server will start.

Two paths matter from the start:

- **`system.toml` itself.** The default location is
  `/etc/herold/system.toml`. Override it with the `--system-config`
  flag or the `HEROLD_SYSTEM_CONFIG` environment variable.
- **The data directory**, set as `data_dir` under `[server]`. This is
  the single directory herold writes everything into -- the database,
  the blob tree, the full-text index, the outbound queue, and ACME
  certificate state. Herold creates it on first start if it does not
  exist.

The Docker image ships a working `system.toml` for loopback testing, so
you can postpone writing your own (see [Install with
Docker](#install-with-docker)). A source install needs the file from
the start.

The [Configuration Reference](configure) documents every setting; the
two decisions you make while writing the file are below.

### Storage backend

{%# REQ-STORE #%}

Herold stores metadata in **SQLite** by default -- pure Go, zero
dependencies, and the right choice for a single host up to a couple of
hundred mailboxes. The database file lives inside `data_dir`.

For larger deployments, or where you already operate Postgres, set
`[server.storage] backend = "postgres"` with a connection string. Both
backends are fully supported. [Operating Herold](operate) covers
switching backends after install.

### TLS

{%# REQ-OPS-40 #%}

Any internet-facing deployment needs TLS for IMAP, JMAP, the web
surfaces, and MTA-STS. Herold has a built-in ACME client: configure
`[acme]` in `system.toml` with a contact email and herold obtains and
renews certificates from Let's Encrypt automatically (HTTP-01,
TLS-ALPN-01, or DNS-01 via a DNS-provider plugin).

If you manage certificates elsewhere -- cert-manager, an internal PKI, a
wildcard certificate -- give each listener an explicit `cert_file` and
`key_file` instead.

_Going Live_ has the full ACME and DNS walkthrough.

## Install with Docker

The official image is published to the GitHub Container Registry. Pull
it:

```bash
docker pull ghcr.io/hanshuebner/herold:latest
```

The image is a small distroless build that runs as the non-root user
`nonroot` (UID 65532). It ships two things that let it boot without any
mounts -- a default `system.toml` at `/etc/herold/system.toml` and a
throwaway self-signed certificate -- which is enough for loopback
testing but not for a real deployment.

For a real deployment, provide your own config and a persistent data
directory:

1. **Create a host data directory** and give it to the container's
   user:

   ```bash
   mkdir -p /srv/herold/data
   chown -R 65532:65532 /srv/herold/data
   ```

2. **Write your `system.toml`** (see [The system
   config](#the-system-config)) and place it on the host, e.g. at
   `/srv/herold/system.toml`. Set `data_dir = "/var/lib/herold"` in it
   -- that is where the data directory is mounted inside the container.

3. **Run the container**, mounting both and publishing the standard
   ports:

   ```bash
   docker run -d --name herold --restart unless-stopped \
     -v /srv/herold/data:/var/lib/herold \
     -v /srv/herold/system.toml:/etc/herold/system.toml:ro \
     -p 25:25 -p 587:587 -p 465:465 \
     -p 143:143 -p 993:993 -p 4190:4190 \
     -p 80:80 -p 443:443 -p 9443:9443 \
     ghcr.io/hanshuebner/herold:latest
   ```

| Port      | Purpose                                          |
|-----------|--------------------------------------------------|
| 25        | Inbound SMTP from other mail servers             |
| 587, 465  | Authenticated mail submission                    |
| 143, 993  | IMAP (993 is implicit TLS)                       |
| 4190      | ManageSieve                                      |
| 80, 443   | Webmail and JMAP on the public listener; 80 also serves ACME HTTP-01 |
| 9443      | Operator UI and admin API on the admin listener  |

To pin a release instead of tracking `latest`, replace the tag
(`ghcr.io/hanshuebner/herold:v0.5.0`). A Compose file is often easier to
maintain than a long `docker run`; the manual ships an example at
_`docs/manual/user/examples/docker-compose.yml`_ to adapt.

The server is running once the container is up. CLI commands run inside
the container -- `docker exec herold /usr/local/bin/herold <args>` --
which the [First run](#first-run) steps use. Continue there.

Prefer to build and run the binary directly? See [Install from
source](#install-from-source) instead.

## Install from source

You need:

- **Go 1.25 or newer.** `go.mod` pins the toolchain, so an older `go`
  command transparently fetches the right version on the first build.
  Air-gapped hosts must install Go 1.25+ from `go.dev/dl/` directly.
- **git** -- to clone the repository.
- A **Linux or macOS** host. Linux is the runtime target; macOS works
  for development.

No C compiler is required: herold's default build is pure Go.

1. **Build the binary:**

   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/hanshuebner/herold.git
   cd herold
   make build
   ```

   `make build` builds the web assets, compiles the binary, and writes
   it to `bin/herold`. Copy it onto your `$PATH`:

   ```bash
   sudo install bin/herold /usr/local/bin/herold
   ```

2. **Place your `system.toml`** (see [The system
   config](#the-system-config)) at `/etc/herold/system.toml`, and make
   sure its `data_dir` points somewhere the herold process can write.

3. **Start the server:**

   ```bash
   herold server start --system-config /etc/herold/system.toml
   ```

   For an unattended host, run herold under a service manager
   (systemd, runit) instead so it starts at boot and restarts on
   failure.

The binary has no runtime dependencies beyond a filesystem and a
network stack. Continue with [First run](#first-run).

For Go-only hosts with no Node toolchain, build customization, and
serving the web UI from disk during development, see [Advanced Build
Options](advanced-builds).

Prefer a container? See [Install with Docker](#install-with-docker)
instead.

## First run

With the server running, bootstrap the first administrator, add a
domain, and add a user.

{% callout type="info" title="Running the herold CLI" %}
On a **source install**, run the `herold` commands below directly. On a
**Docker install**, run them inside the container:
`docker exec herold /usr/local/bin/herold <args>`.
{% /callout %}

To validate a config file without starting the server:

```bash
herold server config-check /etc/herold/system.toml
```

It exits non-zero with an actionable message naming the offending key on
failure. {%# REQ-OPS-06 #%}

### Create the administrator

`herold bootstrap` creates the first principal -- an administrator --
and prints an API key:

```bash
herold bootstrap --email admin@example.com
```

With no `--password` herold generates a strong one and prints it.
**Capture the password and the API key now**: the password is stored
Argon2id-hashed and the key SHA-256-hashed, so neither can be read back
from the server afterwards.

`bootstrap` writes the API key to `~/.herold/credentials.toml`, so
subsequent `herold` commands in the same shell authenticate without
flags. It refuses to run once any principal exists.

### Add your first domain

```bash
herold domain add example.com
```

Herold generates DKIM keys for the domain and assembles the DNS records
it needs -- DKIM, SPF, DMARC, MTA-STS, and TLS reporting. With a DNS
plugin configured it publishes them automatically; otherwise it prints
the records for you to publish by hand. _Going Live_ walks through this
in full. {%# REQ-OPS-60 #%}

### Add a user

```bash
herold principal create user1@example.com
```

User management -- aliases, mailboxes, API keys, granting admin -- is
covered in [Administering Herold](administer).

### Sign in

With a domain and a user in place: {%# REQ-DEPLOY-COLOC-01..05 #%}

- **Webmail** -- the Suite app on the public listener at
  `https://<your host>/`. Users sign in on the page that loads and get
  mail, calendar, contacts, and chat. Password changes, two-factor
  enrolment, and API keys live under `/#/settings`.
- **Operator UI** -- the admin app at `/admin/` on the admin listener
  (port 9443 by default). Expose it through a [reverse
  proxy](reverse-proxy) or reach it over an SSH tunnel.
- **IMAP / SMTP / JMAP** -- the standard ports above. The user manual
  _Quickstart_ has a desktop-client walkthrough.

The server reports readiness on `GET /api/v1/healthz/ready` once every
listener, the store, and all plugins are up. {%# REQ-OPS-110, REQ-OPS-111 #%}

